## Recommended: Adopt `System.IO.Abstractions`

This is the established NuGet package for making filesystem statics testable — no custom wrappers needed.

### 1. Install the package

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
// In Program.cs or Startup.cs:
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
public class MyService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Read(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Write(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool EnsureDirectory(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```bash
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

var service = new MyService(mockFs);

Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", service.Read("/data/config.json"));

service.Write("/data/output.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/data/output.txt"));
```

---

**Why this over a custom wrapper?**
- Covers the entire `System.IO` surface (File, Directory, Path, etc.)
- Battle-tested, actively maintained
- `MockFileSystem` gives you an in-memory filesystem for tests — no temp files, no cleanup

Once installed and registered, you can use the `migrate-static-to-wrapper` skill to bulk-replace all existing `File.*` / `Directory.*` call sites with the injected `IFileSystem`.
